{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB13064 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Tramazoline appears to be associated with Vasomotor Rhinitis through a chain of indications that involve inflammatory and vasodilatory pathways. The evidence suggests that Tramazoline may act as a mediator in the development of Vasomotor Rhinitis by influencing the inflammatory response in the nasal passages. Specifically, Tramazoline is linked to Nasopharyngitis, which in turn is associated with Potassium Iodide and Clofedanol, both of which are known to contribute to vasomotor symptoms. While the exact mechanism by which Tramazoline influences these pathways remains unclear, the cumulative evidence points to a potential role in modulating inflammatory and vascular responses that are characteristic of Vasomotor Rhinitis. The integration of these indications highlights a possible pathway where Tramazoline could exert its effects through indirect interactions, potentially involving the modulation of inflammatory mediators or vascular tone.",
